      "The 1889 fire started on the evening of 4 July in a building on Main Street and spread through the wooden business district in hours, helped by a stiff wind and a water system that could not keep up. Around ten blocks burned. Merchants rebuilt quickly in brick and stone, and the Davidson Building and other ornate commercial blocks from that rebuilding still give downtown its late-Victorian look.",
      "Washoe arrived at Central Washington University in 1980 with the researchers Roger and Deborah Fouts, who founded a chimpanzee and human communication institute there. Washoe and her adopted son Loulis, along with several other chimps, signed with one another and with their human companions; Loulis was observed learning signs from Washoe rather than from people. Washoe died in Ellensburg in 2007, and the remaining chimpanzees were later moved to a sanctuary.",
      "The valley's other treasure is a stone. Ellensburg Blue, a rare sky-blue agate, is found only in the hills north of town and has been prized by collectors since the early twentieth century; it sells for many times the price of ordinary agate. Rockhounds still walk the ridges after spring thaws looking for it.",
      "Irrigation from the Yakima Project turned much of the valley floor into fields of timothy hay, much of it pressed into bales and shipped across the Pacific to feed livestock in Asia. The same dry, windy climate that cures hay so well also makes the grasslands around town quick to burn, and the wind that powers the turbines on the ridges is the one that throws embers ahead of a fire.",
